                 (PnMeshState->mqtt_map_report_settings).
                 Notes on TODO transcription slips found while
                 descoping: "Status Message" is not a real
                 ModuleConfig sub-block; "Traffic Management"
                 is rebroadcast_mode inside DeviceConfig
                 (covered by Phase 14); "Display" is a Config
                 sub-block (was covered by Phase 13, now also
                 postponed -- see below).

    [x] Phase 12 Position + Power config tabs.  Shipped as two
                 new GtkExpanders distributed across the existing
                 7-tab layout rather than dedicated tabs -- Power
                 sits under "Device" alongside Identity, Position
                 sits under "Radio" after Region / Channels / Share.
                 Both reuse the LoRa pattern: read whole sub-block
                 during the handshake, mutate one or more fields,
                 ship every typed field back at once via
                 AM_SET_CONFIG=34 wrapped in Config{<sub>}.

                 Wire side (pn-mesh-connection.[ch]): CFG_POSITION
                 =2 and CFG_POWER=3 dispatch in parse_config; POS_*
                 / POW_* field defines mirror the Meshtastic proto;
                 parse_position_config / parse_power_config follow
                 parse_lora_config's shape.  PnMeshState gains 13
                 pos_* and 9 pow_* fields plus have_position /
                 have_power flags.  Two new writer pairs --
                 PnMeshPositionConfigWrite + set_position_config_*,
                 PnMeshPowerConfigWrite + set_power_config_* --
                 share two helpers (send_set_config_subblock and
                 finish_set_config) extracted from set_lora_config
                 so the second writer collapses to a per-field
                 write loop + one call.  Power's
                 adc_multiplier_override is the project's first
                 float-on-the-wire field; written via
                 pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union punning
                 trick, read via pn_mesh_pb_read_float.

                 UI side: pn-mesh-page-position.[c,h] and
                 pn-mesh-page-power.[c,h] are direct clones of
                 pn-mesh-page-region.[c,h]: subtitle + grid of
                 bold-label / control rows + right-aligned Apply
                 button at the bottom; ctx struct with all widget
                 handles + status / busy callbacks + writing flag;
                 set_writing toggles every control's sensitivity in
                 one shot and emits busy(TRUE/FALSE) on transition;
                 set_state mirrors the device state into the
                 controls and stashes round-trip-only fields
                 (position_flags, the GPIOs, the legacy gps_enabled
                 vs gps_mode pair, the INA address) in ctx so Apply
                 ships them back unchanged.

                 Position page exposes GPS mode (combo: DISABLED /
                 ENABLED / NOT_PRESENT), the legacy gps_enabled
                 boolean synced from mode at Apply-time so older
                 firmware keeps consistent state, fixed_position
                 switch, smart-broadcast switch + its min-distance
                 (m) and min-interval (s) thresholds, plus
                 position_broadcast_secs and gps_update_interval.
                 GPIO assignments are deliberately NOT surfaced as
                 controls -- they are board-specific and the user
                 is more likely to set them via the firmware
                 console than the dialog; round-trip preserves
                 them anyway.

                 Power page exposes is_power_saving switch,
                 on_battery_shutdown_after_secs,
                 adc_multiplier_override (float spinner, 3-digit
                 precision, 0 = firmware default),
                 wait_bluetooth_secs, sds_secs, ls_secs,
                 min_wake_secs; device_battery_ina_address is
                 round-tripped only.  powermon_enables (field 32)
                 is a debug-only flag the dialog ignores -- same
                 caveat the LoRa page carries for spread_factor
                 etc.  It gets proto3-zero-reset on Apply (rare;
                 no end-user touched it via the GUI), document
                 if a user reports it.

                 Gotchas for the next Config sub-block writer
                 (Phase 14):

                 (i) Float on the wire uses
                 pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union to
                 reinterpret bits; pn_mesh_pb_read_float
                 reciprocates.  Do NOT write a varint -- proto's
                 float wire type is fixed32 (wire 5), and a varint
                 of the bit pattern would be read as garbage.

                 (ii) Signed int32 fields on the wire encode
                 negative values as 10-byte sign-extended varints.
                 The dialog's smart-min-distance spinner clamps to
                 0 so the path is never hit in practice, but the
                 writer still casts gint32 -> gint64 -> guint64
                 before pn_mesh_pb_write_varint_field so the
                 contract is correct for any signed field added
                 later.

                 (iii) The pattern is: parse all fields the
                 firmware sends, expose only the practically-
                 useful ones in the UI, stash the rest in ctx and
                 ship them back unchanged.  This avoids the
                 proto3-defaults trap and keeps board-specific /
                 advanced settings intact without bloating the
                 page.

    [-] Phase 13 Display + Bluetooth config tabs.  POSTPONED
                 to a later revision (decided 2026-05-28) --
                 lower payoff than Phases 7/12/14 for the unbox
                 flow; revisit alongside any Phase 11 module
                 work.  DisplayConfig (13 fields: screen_on_secs,
                 flip_screen, units, use_12h_clock, ...) and
                 BluetoothConfig (3 fields: enabled, mode,
                 fixed_pin).

    [x] Phase 14 Device + Network + Security config tabs.
                 Three new pages following the LoRa/Position/Power
                 set_config_subblock pattern: parse the whole
                 sub-block during the handshake, ship every typed
                 field back at once via AM_SET_CONFIG=34 wrapped in
                 Config{<sub>}.  Reuses send_set_config_subblock +
                 finish_set_config helpers from Phase 12.

                 Tab layout (touched 3 of the 7 existing tabs;
                 Mesh tools + Notifications + Telemetry +
                 Diagnostics unchanged):
                   Device:  Identity / Device role & GPIO (new) /
                            Power / Security (new)
                   Radio:   Region & LoRa / Channels / Share /
                            Position
                   Network: WiFi/Ethernet/IPv6 (new) / MQTT
                            (placeholder dropped)

                 Wire side (pn-mesh-connection.[ch]): CFG_DEVICE=1
                 / CFG_NETWORK=4 / CFG_SECURITY=8 dispatch in
                 parse_config; DEV_*/NET_*/SEC_* field defines
                 mirror the Meshtastic Config.proto exactly.
                 parse_device_config / parse_network_config /
                 parse_security_config follow parse_lora_config's
                 shape.  PnMeshState gains 13 dev_*, 10 net_*,
                 7 sec_* fields plus have_device/have_network/
                 have_security flags.  Three writer pairs --
                 PnMeshDeviceConfigWrite + set_device_config_*,
                 PnMeshNetworkConfigWrite + set_network_config_*,
                 PnMeshSecurityConfigWrite + set_security_config_*
                 -- all use the shared subblock helpers.

                 Device page: Role + RebroadcastMode combos,
                 NodeInfo-broadcast spinner, button/buzzer GPIO
                 spinners.  Round-trips serial_enabled, double_
                 tap_as_button_press, deprecated is_managed,
                 disable_triple_click, tzdef, led_heartbeat_
                 disabled, buzzer_mode unchanged.

                 Network page: WiFi switch + SSID/PSK entries
                 (PSK masked), Ethernet switch, IPv6 switch.
                 Round-trips ntp_server, address_mode,
                 ipv4_config (opaque GBytes, same pattern as
                 mqtt_map_report_settings), rsyslog_server,
                 enabled_protocols.

                 Security page: read-only base64 view of the
                 X25519 public/private keys + admin key list,
                 switches for is_managed / serial_enabled /
                 debug_log_api_enabled / admin_channel_enabled.
                 Per TODO: "I understand the risks" check gates
                 Apply, and the check is auto-reset after a
                 successful Apply / on every set_state so the
                 next write needs an explicit re-confirmation.
                 Key bytes shipped back verbatim from PnMeshState
                 (typing 32 random bytes by hand is not useful).

                 Files shipped: lib/pn-mesh-page-device.{c,h},
                 lib/pn-mesh-page-network.{c,h},
                 lib/pn-mesh-page-security.{c,h}; deltas in
                 lib/pn-mesh-connection.{c,h}, lib/pn-mesh-dialog.c,
                 lib/Makefile.am.

                 Gotchas for any future Config sub-block writer
                 (none yet planned; Phase 11/13 are postponed):

                 (i) BYTES round-trip via GBytes.  ipv4_config,
                 public_key, private_key are length-delimited
                 bytes sub-fields.  Stash them on PnMeshState as
                 GBytes* (own-on-parse, free-on-close, clear in
                 clear_state) and ref+copy into a per-call struct
                 in the async wrapper so the worker thread sees a
                 stable input even if the page rebuilds its ctx
                 mid-flight.

                 (ii) REPEATED bytes (admin_key).  Same recipe but
                 a GPtrArray-of-GBytes with g_bytes_unref as the
                 free func, allocated up front in open_sync and
                 set_size(0) at the top of each parse pass.  The
                 async wrapper deep-copies the array (g_bytes_ref
                 each entry) before handing it to the thread.

                 (iii) "I understand" check.  Reset to FALSE on
                 every set_state and after a successful Apply --
                 a sticky check would let a user accidentally
                 re-Apply a stale config after a handshake gave
                 fresh data.

Phase 8 (Plugin extraction) is no longer tracked under TODO #29 --
moved out 2026-05-28: it is its own cross-cutting project (Devices
menu becomes plugin-driven via a new PnDeviceProvider core API; the
meshtastic dialog code migrates into the meshtastic plugin's GUI
companion; PnMeshtastic nodes gain a "Set up device..." affordance
that pre-picks the device).  Will get its own TODO entry when picked
up.

Gotchas worth re-reading before each phase (from pip-mesh, costly to
rediscover): always drain stale bytes before any handshake; never
wait for an end marker, wait for silence (3s config, 5s metadata);
sleep 0.5 after every write; bare set_channel is no-reboot,
commit_edit_settings persists+may reboot -- transactional only when
needed; cache invalidation keyed on USB VID:PID (the tty name gets
reused); frame magic is 0x94 0xC3, length is 2-byte BIG-endian;
short-name <=4 chars, long-name <=39, PSK exactly 32 or 64 hex chars.
The pip-mesh --help string flags --list-messages as broken; trust
the help text not the man page.

Hard rules (do not inherit from pip-mesh): no ~/.cache/, no
auto-detect on dialog open, no "auto-select if exactly one device",
no --raw or --max-messages CLI modes leaking into the GUI, no global
HEXDATA/PB_POS state -- each PnMeshConnection holds its own.

(30) MQTT node hardening (from the 2026-05-28 code review of
lib/pn-mqtt.c/.h, lib/pn-mqtt-sink.c/.h, lib/pn-mqtt-profile.c/.h and
tests/unit/test-pn-mqtt*.c).  The nodes are sound and GTK-free; these
are the findings worth fixing.  NOTE: pn-mesh-page-mqtt.c is the
Meshtastic radio's MQTT-gateway page (#29), NOT one of these nodes.

  CRITICAL
  - [x] C1  Sink reports every failure only via g_warning/g_message ->
        invisible from the desktop launcher (violates the "errors via
        failure message, not stdout" rule; cf. pn-http.c:272).  Offenders
        in pn-mqtt-sink.c: queued-publish fail :486, live publish fail
        :671, connect refused :564, invalid URL :731, mosquitto_new/
        connect_async/loop_start/username_pw_set/tls_set :744,766,774,
        782,795.  Emit success=FALSE + human-readable output instead.
        (Source has the same smell -- see L1.)
        VISUAL ERROR INDICATION DONE 2026-05-29: added a GENERIC node
        error state to the core -- pn_node_set_has_error()/
        pn_node_get_has_error() + "has-error" property on PnNode
        (transient, not serialized, not shown in the dialog).  The
        worksheet painter (draw_node) now paints ANY node with has-error
        set red + ❗, exactly like it greys `disabled` nodes; disabled
        wins over error.  So error indication is uniform and free for
        every node type -- the ~25 other nodes that still hand-roll their
        own red+❗ WARNING_ICON swap (pn-http, pn-ws, pn-tts, tasmota/*,
        shell/*, network/*, ...) are candidates to migrate later.  MQTT
        Source + Sink now drop their bespoke colour/icon swap and just
        toggle has-error from apply_visual_state; the Sink gained a
        sticky publish_error flag (set on a rejected live/queued publish,
        cleared on the next good publish or reconnect) so a publish that
        fails while the session is up turns the node red instead of
        silently dropping the message.  Source subscribe-failure now goes
        red instead of green (also closes L1).
        TEXTUAL-REASON MECHANISM DONE 2026-05-29 (#32): the agreed
        per-node "log" dialog now exists -- every PnNode carries a log
        ring (pn_node_log) and the right-click menu opens a live console
        (PnLogDialog).
        C1 NOW CLOSED 2026-05-29: pn-mqtt.c and pn-mqtt-sink.c report their
        failures through pn_node_log so the REASON is readable in the node's
        Log dialog.  restart_client failures (main thread) log directly; the
        net-thread reasons (connect refused / subscribe failed / disconnect /
        connected, plus the Sink's live & queued publish failures) ride the
        existing main-thread conn-state trampoline (post_conn_state_on_main_
        full) so log-changed is emitted on the main loop.  The g_warning/
        g_message calls were then REMOVED (the user did not want the stderr
        noise once the Log dialog covered it) -- pn_node_log is the sole
        reporting channel for these nodes now.  (The Source could ALSO
        emit success=FALSE downstream per the Weather pattern -- not needed
        now that the log dialog covers both Source and the output-less Sink.)
  - [x] C2  restart_client() runs from _init() (pn-mqtt.c:1173,
        pn-mqtt-sink.c:1154), before construction/deserialization is done.
        Source: starts the network thread before a SUBCLASS (Sink,
        Tasmota, Zigbee2MQTT) has initialized its private state, yet
        on_mqtt_message calls klass->accept_topic() on the net thread.
        Sink: every credential setter (broker-profile/url/username/
        password/client-id, :854,859,875,880,885) re-runs restart_client,
        so loading a document fires ~5 full connect cycles in a burst.
        Fix: defer the initial connect to constructed()/g_idle and
        debounce reconnects into one idle instead of reconnecting
        synchronously in each setter.
        DONE 2026-05-29: both nodes gained schedule_restart() — a debounced
        g_idle (restart_idle_id) that coalesces a burst of setter / vault-
        change calls into ONE restart_client run.  The _init() call is gone;
        the initial connect is scheduled from constructed() (after the migrate
        idle, so a broker-profile change the migration makes folds into the
        same restart) and thus runs only after deserialization + subclass init
        are complete.  restart_idle_id is cancelled in dispose.  The Sink's
        offline queue means a message arriving before the deferred connect is
        buffered + flushed, so deferring loses nothing.  (test-pn-mqtt-sink's
        error_funnel_subclass now pumps the main loop to let the debounced
        restart run.)

  HIGH
  - [x] H1  Network-thread read of priv->topic/priv->qos races runtime
        reconfigure (pn-mqtt.c:467-469).  The "no mutex for config fields"
        invariant assumes setters always stop_client (join the thread)
        first, but libmosquitto's AUTO-RECONNECT keeps the net thread
        alive and re-fires on_mqtt_connect with no stop_client -- so a
        reconnect can read priv->topic while a setter frees/replaces it
        (torn read / UAF).  Fix: snapshot topic/qos at connect time or
        guard with the existing lock.
        DONE 2026-05-29 (Source only; the Sink has no net-thread topic read):
        restart_client snapshots priv->topic/qos into priv->sub_topic/sub_qos
        on the main thread (after stop_client has joined the previous thread,
        before loop_start), and on_mqtt_connect re-subscribes from the
        snapshot, never the live property.  The snapshot is fixed for a
        client's lifetime and only replaced after the next stop_client joins
        the thread, so an auto-reconnect re-firing on_mqtt_connect cannot race
        a setter freeing priv->topic.  sub_topic is owned, freed in
        stop_client + finalize.
  - [x] H2  Stale-disconnect race during reconnect-while-restarting, present
        EQUALLY in both nodes.  The conn-state trampoline guards only on
        client != NULL (pn-mqtt.c:413, pn-mqtt-sink.c:514); neither has a
        real generation/epoch counter, so an old session's queued
        trampoline running after restart_client installs a new client can
        flip a live node back to red (and on the Source skip the genuine
        reconnect's re-subscribe).  Fix: capture a generation counter in
        the closure, compare in the trampoline.
        DONE 2026-05-29: each client carries a heap PnMqtt[Sink]NetCtx
        {self, generation} as its mosquitto_new user data instead of bare
        `self`; restart_client bumps priv->generation per client and stamps it
        on the ctx.  The net-thread callbacks read THEIR generation off the
        ctx (no racy read of priv->generation) and pass it into the conn-state
        closure; the trampoline honours an update only when client != NULL AND
        c->generation == priv->generation, so a stale session's queued
        trampoline is dropped.  net_ctx is owned in priv and freed when its
        client is destroyed (stop_client / restart's fail path), after the
        thread is joined.
  - [x] H3  Sink drops buffered startup commands on flush failure:
        flush_pending (pn-mqtt-sink.c:476-492) pending_free's each entry
        unconditionally even when mosquitto_publish errored (:485),
        defeating the buffer-and-flush guarantee.  Fix: on NOMEM/CONN_LOST
        re-queue rather than discard.
        DONE 2026-05-29: flush_pending now classifies the publish error.  On a
        TRANSIENT error (MOSQ_ERR_NOMEM / CONN_LOST / NO_CONN — the link
        dropped mid-flush) it re-queues the failed entry AND every still-
        unflushed one (preserving order + original timestamps, by detaching
        them from the collect_fresh array with index = NULL so the array's
        free func skips them) and stops, so a buffered startup command
        survives to the next reconnect.  A PERMANENT error (payload too large,
        protocol) still drops that one entry and continues, since re-queueing
        it would loop forever.

  MEDIUM
  - [x] M1  build_mqtt_message (pn-mqtt.c:592-598) mirrors only DOUBLE/INT64
        JSON scalars to data.value; a bare true/false payload sets nothing
        (breaks the 0.0/1.0 boolean convention), and int64 loses precision
        via get_double.  Add a JSON-boolean -> 0.0/1.0 case.
        DONE 2026-05-29: pn_mqtt_build_message (now in pn-mqtt-util.c) gained a
        G_TYPE_BOOLEAN arm in the scalar->value mirror — false->0.0, true->1.0
        per the project boolean convention.  test-pn-mqtt-util's old
        message_json_bool_no_value (which PINNED the missing mirror) is now
        message_json_bool_value, asserting 1.0/0.0.  (int64 precision left as
        is — data.value is a double by contract.)
  - [ ] M2  Sink loses buffered messages on dispose (finalize :989) and on a
        runtime URL change keeps the backlog and flushes commands meant
        for broker A to broker B.  Decide + document; likely clear the
        queue when broker identity changes.
  - [ ] M3  Profile lacks TLS/CA-file, port-as-int, and keepalive fields.
        TLS is inferred from URL scheme only and mosquitto_tls_set(...NULL)
        uses the system trust store (pn-mqtt.c:771), so a PRIVATE-CA broker
        is unconfigurable; keepalive hard-coded to 60s.  Known/deferred.
  - [ ] M4  payload_len (gsize) cast to int at pn-mqtt-sink.c:483,666; >256MB
        hits MQTT's limit and mosquitto_publish rejects -- logged only to
        stderr (see C1).

  LOW / NIT
  - [x] L1  Source: subscribe failure (pn-mqtt.c:469-475) g_warns then flips
        the node GREEN anyway -> looks healthy, receives nothing.
  - [x] L2  Dead variable need_schedule (pn-mqtt.c:375,388,394).
        DONE 2026-05-29: removed from emit_message_on_main.
  - [ ] L3  Default topic "#" + a primary broker profile = a freshly dropped
        node auto-subscribes to the entire broker (footgun, intentional).
  - [ ] L4  Empty-payload publish is dropped (pn-mqtt-sink.c:383) -> cannot
        clear a retained topic with a null payload; document in help.
  - [x] N1  restart_client repeats the g_free(url/user/pass/cid/host) cleanup
        block ~5x per file; an out: goto would remove the divergence risk.
        DONE 2026-05-29: both restart_client bodies now use a single out:
        label for the free + apply_visual_state tail and a fail_destroy:
        label for the post-mosquitto_new failure paths (destroy client + free
        net_ctx).  The ~5 duplicated cleanup blocks are gone.
  - [x] N2  Stale header docs: pn-mqtt.h says property "topic" but the
        installed name is "subscribe-topic" (:1101); pn-mqtt-sink.h omits
        broker-profile/topic-template.
        DONE 2026-05-29: pn-mqtt.h Properties block now lists broker-profile
        + subscribe-topic (was "topic"); pn-mqtt-sink.h adds broker-profile +
        topic-template and tags url as the inline "Custom settings" field.
  - [x] N3  parse_mqtt_url is DUPLICATED across both .c files (divergence
        risk); a shared static + shared test would cover both.
        DONE 2026-05-28: extracted to pn_mqtt_parse_url in pn-mqtt-util.c,
        both nodes call it.
  - [x] N4  pn_mqtt_parse_url mis-parses IPv6 literals: it locates the port
        with strchr(p, ':'), so tcp://[::1]:1883 splits on the first ':'
        inside the address.  Bracketed-IPv6 host[:port] is unsupported;
        handle '[...]' before scanning for the port colon (then add a
        parse-table case -- today's behaviour is a bug, so don't pin it).
        DONE 2026-05-29: pn_mqtt_parse_url handles a leading '[' — host is the
        bracket contents, an optional ":port" may follow the ']', and an
        unterminated/empty/junk-after form is rejected.  Port parsing factored
        into a parse_port_str helper (also requires the port token be fully
        consumed, *endp=='\0').  New test-pn-mqtt-util url_ipv6 case covers
        tcp://[::1], [::1], mqtts://[2001:db8::1], ...:port, and the malformed
        rejections.
  - [x] U1  Inline Broker URL silently ignored once any mqtt-broker profile
        exists: empty broker-profile -> pn_node_get_profile falls back to
        the PRIMARY profile, and pn_mqtt_resolve_connection takes every
        field from a non-NULL profile, so a typed url did nothing (user
        report 2026-05-29: "no matter what I write to Broker URL it keeps
        using the saved broker").
        DONE 2026-05-29 (final model = explicit "Custom settings"): the Broker
        profile picker gained a third choice.  broker-profile now has three
        states -- "" = Default/primary profile, "<id>" = that profile, and the
        reserved sentinel PN_PROFILE_REF_CUSTOM ("@custom") = "Custom settings",
        i.e. use the node's own inline url/credentials.  pn_node_get_profile()
        returns NULL for the sentinel (no primary fallback), so resolution is
        back to the simple profile-XOR-inline (pn_mqtt_resolve_connection
        reverted from the interim per-field variant).  Applies to BOTH Source
        and Sink.  Opt-in via pn_param_spec_set_profile_ref_inline_fields(pspec,
        "url,username,password,client-id") on the shared profile-ref combo in
        pn-node-dialog.c, which adds the "Custom settings" entry (LAST in the
        list).
        SAVE/LOAD INVARIANT + GATING (2026-05-29 follow-up): inline custom data
        exists ONLY in "Custom settings" mode.  (a) The combo greys out the
        tagged inline-field editors unless "@custom" is selected (find-by-name
        "pn-prop-<field>" under the grid; applied on combo "map" + each
        populate).  (b) Picking Default/a profile clears those fields ("").  (c)
        On load, migrate_legacy_credentials clears stale inline data when a real
        profile id is selected, keeps it for "@custom", and for "" still does the
        legacy creds-import / url->@custom (a new-model Default node has empty
        inline fields, so that flip only fires for genuinely legacy files --
        distinguished by url emptiness, no extra flag).  This makes the picker's
        choice survive save->load reliably (the reported bug: leftover inline
        url re-triggered Custom on load).  The earlier "editing url auto-selects
        Custom" was removed -- a greyed field can't be edited; the user picks
        Custom via the combo.  Bundled example mqtt-test.json set to "@custom".
        Covered by test-pn-mqtt-resolve.c (profile_wins, custom_sentinel_uses_
        inline); clearing/gating are dialog/idle behavior, verified manually.
        Docs: url property blurb + PnMqtt.html / PnMqttSink.html.
        (History: first "explicit profile wins / typed url overrides primary",
        then per-field, then this explicit-mode model + the save/load invariant.)

